Versions of Super ZZ Zero are using semantic version numbers. If the major
version number is 0 then that means that version 1 is not finished yet and
that there is the possibility (although unlikely) that any minor version
number change can cause incompatibilities. If the major version is nonzero
then changes that do not affect the major version number should remain
backward compatible with previous versions of Super ZZ Zero that have the
same major version number, unless there is a significant bug (but in such
a case, it will be considered if that would potentially damage existing
worlds and if so, a check will be made at run time and the compatibility
will be enabled according to the GENERAL.DER file), or if the world causes
a deliberate error condition, or if a world uses an undocumented feature
which is also unintentional. If only the patch number is changed, then it
should be backward and forward compatible, although bugs which do not
affect game play might be corrected.

If you are making your own experimental or incompatible versions of Super
ZZ Zero, then you must change the version.c file to use different OIDs,
since relative OIDs starting with 0 are reserved for official versions.

The version.status file is used for managing the versions. The first line
contains the semantic version number. The second line contains a hash
which is calculated by the use of all of the files listed in all of the
rest of the lines in this file. If another file is added here, then you
also must add it into the appropriate line of main.mak so that it will
know to update version.status and version.inc when it is compiled. Any
file that affects the operation of the compiled program should be included.

The version.inc file is auto-generated from version.status. You should not
modify it; you should modify version.c instead, please.

You can run "superzz0 -V" in order to check the current version.

The version hash is not currently used by Super ZZ Zero, but might later
be used in the HISTORY.DER file (if this feature is enabled in the
configuration file) for the purpose of keeping track of which version(s)
of Super ZZ Zero have been used for editing this world file (the OID will
also be recorded), in case it will somehow help to figure out what went
wrong if upgrading the version of Super ZZ Zero causes something to not
work properly.

